{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0184vXNG3gpAkdHVaw58kEKT","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Decision Log — K-12 Market Intelligence Harness\n2\t\n3\t> Architecture Decision Records. Append-only. Each records a decision, why, and what it\n4\t> changed. Once logged, decisions are final — don't relitigate without a new ADR\n5\t> superseding the old one. Where an ADR carries a decision proven in the predecessor\n6\t> project, it says so; the predecessor repo (`k-12MarketIntelligence`, archived) holds\n7\t> the full history, and nothing here depends on reading it.\n8\t\n9\tFormat: **Context** · **Decision** · **Consequences**.\n10\t\n11\t---\n12\t\n13\t## ADR-001 — The evidence contract is the only data interface\n14\t\n15\t**Context.** The data layer lives in a separate project that publishes a versioned\n16\tdeterministic evidence contract (question registry + response schemas + CLI emitter,\n17\tvendored under `contract/`). The predecessor harness design had an LLM composing SQL\n18\tover a read-only warehouse schema; that entire concern — schemas, joins, identity\n19\tresolution, data quality — moved behind the data project's contract.\n20\t**Decision.** The harness's only data path is the contract: structured requests to\n21\tapproved question IDs via the emitter CLI, run in the data project's own environment.\n22\tNo SQL, no database connection, no raw assets, no name-matching across source systems,\n23\tno arithmetic in the model — every number in every answer originates in the envelope's\n24\t`result`/`evidence`.\n25\t**Consequences.** The harness becomes an intent mapper, slot filler, and faithful\n26\trenderer. The answerable set is bounded by the registry: growing it is a contract\n27\trelease, not a harness change. Supersedes the predecessor's text-to-SQL agent design\n28\t(its ADR-0002/0013) — recorded here once so the history is carried without being\n29\tload-bearing.\n30\t\n31\t---\n32\t\n33\t## ADR-002 — PydanticAI (slim, Anthropic-only, pinned exactly); union output with tools\n34\t\n35\t**Context.** The product returns several distinct typed shapes, and the agent must call\n36\ttools and return typed output in the same loop. Proven in the predecessor: Instructor's\n37\t`response_model` rewrites the tool array and evicts action tools — the model then fills\n38\ta validating schema with invented values.\n39\t**Decision.** `pydantic-ai-slim[anthropic]`, pinned exactly in `requirements.txt`; a\n40\tunion `output_type` coexisting with custom tools; agent-loop code kept thin so a\n41\tframework swap stays cheap.\n42\t**Consequences.** Adding an answer category is a new union branch, not a loop change.\n43\tThe one framework dep is pinned; other deps stay loose (reproducible-enough for a\n44\tsingle-author project; revisit a lockfile if a transitive break bites).\n45\t\n46\t---\n47\t\n48\t## ADR-003 — Single retry owner: Tenacity, SDK retries disabled\n49\t\n50\t**Context.** The Anthropic SDK retries internally (`max_retries=2` default); a second\n51\tretry layer multiplies requests and drains rate-limit buckets ~3× on 429/529.\n52\t**Decision.** `harness/retry.py` owns all transport retries; the SDK client is\n53\tconstructed with `max_retries=0` (`ANTHROPIC_MAX_RETRIES` in settings). Classification\n54\tlives on the exception hierarchy (`harness/errors.py`): transient retried, terminal and\n55\tauth fail fast.\n56\t**Consequences.** One place for backoff, jitter, and structured retry logging. Never\n57\tadd another retry layer — including around the evidence subprocess, which gets a\n58\ttimeout, not retries (a deterministic emitter that failed once fails again).\n59\t\n60\t---\n61\t\n62\t## ADR-004 — Control envelope enforced in code; typed `BudgetExceeded`\n63\t\n64\t**Context.** Agent loops can run away: steps, tool calls, wall clock, spend.\n65\t**Decision.** `ask()` enforces `UsageLimits` (steps/tool calls) + `asyncio.wait_for`\n66\t(wall clock) + measured spend (`LOOP_MAX_*` in settings); the evidence subprocess has\n67\tits own timeout. A breach returns a typed `BudgetExceeded` carrying what was learned so\n68\tfar — produced by the harness, never selected by the model, never a crash.\n69\t**Consequences.** Worst-case latency and cost are bounded per call. The agent's\n70\t`output_type` stays the success branches; `run_harness()` widens the return type.\n71\t\n72\t---\n73\t\n74\t## ADR-005 — Usage/cost telemetry: JSONL append + DuckDB views at read time\n75\t\n76\t**Context.** Cost work needs per-request granularity (token quartet, USD, latency,\n77\tcache hits) without a hosted tracing service, and telemetry must not couple to any\n78\tother store.\n79\t**Decision.** Each LLM request appends one JSON line to\n80\t`logs/usage/usage-YYYY-MM-DD.jsonl` plus one run-summary line per run; reporting spins\n81\tup an **in-memory** DuckDB over the files (`sql/usage_views.sql`;\n82\t`python -m harness.usage report`). `record_run()` never raises — telemetry failure\n83\tcannot fail a successful run. Costs come from genai-prices (ships with the framework).\n84\t**Consequences.** Near-zero overhead, $0 ongoing cost; cache-hit regression checking is\n85\ta one-line query. When this becomes served/multi-user, add a nullable `consumer_id`\n86\tvia a `schema_version` bump — designed in, not bolted on.\n87\t\n88\t---\n89\t\n90\t## ADR-006 — Frozen cached system prompt; per-model cache minimums; schema block generated from the contract\n91\t\n92\t**Context.** Prompt caching needs a byte-exact prefix, and each model has its own\n93\tminimum cacheable length (values have shifted between models before — verify, don't\n94\tassume). The prompt must describe the question registry, which changes only with a\n95\tcontract release.\n96\t**Decision.** One frozen prompt (`prompts/system.md`): rules block + a schema block\n97\t**generated from `contract/`** at build time (per question: ID, purpose,\n98\trequired/material/optional inputs, result kind; plus the input catalog's allow-lists).\n99\tNothing request-scoped enters the cached prefix. `CACHE_MIN_TOKENS` in settings is the\n100\tsingle source of per-model minimums, re-verified on model upgrades; the regression\n101\tcheck is `cache_read_tokens > 0` in telemetry.\n102\t**Consequences.** Prompt and contract cannot drift — regenerating the schema block is a\n103\tstep of every contract bump (ADR-008). Editing the prompt invalidates the cache; batch\n104\tprompt changes deliberately.\n105\t\n106\t---\n107\t\n108\t## ADR-007 — Eval-first: the golden suite exists before the agent answers a real question\n109\t\n110\t**Context.** The system under test is non-deterministic; single-run assertions and\n111\tgrading the agent's self-report both fail. Proven in the predecessor: deterministic\n112\tgraders reading the transcript, k-trial live runs reporting pass@k (capability) and\n113\tpass^k (reliability).\n114\t**Decision.** Golden cases (NL phrasing → expected question ID + request + direction:\n115\tanswer / clarify / refuse / resist-injection) run against a **stub emitter** — a small\n116\tscript satisfying the same CLI contract, serving canned envelopes and canned exit-2\n117\terrors from fixtures. Deterministic graders: chosen-question match, request match,\n118\tenvelope preservation, grounding (every number in prose traces to the envelope),\n119\tclarification correctness, refusal correctness, canary non-reproduction. Deterministic\n120\ttier gates every PR at zero network cost; the live tier (real credentials, real\n121\temitter) is manual/nightly, never a PR gate. Refusal cases are generated from the\n122\tcontract's exclusion lists.\n123\t**Consequences.** Cases are authored and graded before the agent exists (M2 before M3);\n124\tadding a capability means adding cases. Live transcripts feed error analysis that grows\n125\tthe suite.\n126\t\n127\t---\n128\t\n129\t## ADR-008 — The contract is pinned by hash; a bump is a loud failure\n130\t\n131\t**Context.** The contract is the one interface this system has, and it is owned by\n132\tanother project. Silent drift — a changed schema, a reworded limitation, a new\n133\tregistry version — must not be absorbable by accident.\n134\t**Decision.** `contract/PINNED.json` records the SHA-256 of every vendored contract\n135\tfile; `tests/unit/test_contract_pin.py` recomputes them on every run and asserts the\n136\tregistry's version equals `settings.CONTRACT_VERSION`. Adopting a new contract version\n137\tis one deliberate change: re-vendor, re-pin, regenerate the prompt schema block\n138\t(ADR-006), delete any interim shims the new version obsoletes, re-point golden cases.\n139\t**Consequences.** A v0.2.0 drop fails the suite by design until adopted. The pin is\n140\tnever edited to make the test pass.\n141\t\n142\t---\n143\t\n144\t## ADR-009 — Confidence and limitations render as the data layer states them; the harness never assigns a grade\n145\t\n146\t**Context.** The product's published confidence vocabulary is a frozen five-grade\n147\ttaxonomy (Observed / Derived / Modelled / Indicative / Unsupported — PRD §6.2),\n148\tassigned by rule from properties of the data. The contract v0.2.0 envelope carries four\n149\taxis ratings but no grade; the mapping is agreed to be the **data project's** to own\n150\t(change request CR-4 in `contract/HARNESS_FEEDBACK_v0.1.0.md`; ordering, composition\n151\tlaw and rule identifiers agreed in `contract/HARNESS_RESPONSE_v0.2.0.md` §3; composition\n152\tby operation, the backtest-eligibility lifecycle, and the `claim_eligibility` field — in\n153\tplace of any coverage-driven grade change — agreed in\n154\t`contract/HARNESS_REPLY_TO_DATA_DISCOVERY_v0.2.0.md`).\n155\t**Decision.** The harness renders confidence exactly as the envelope states it —\n156\tinterim: the four axis ratings verbatim and **no** five-grade label; post-CR-4: the\n157\tenvelope's grade and weakest-input, verbatim. Limitations strings render verbatim,\n158\tnever paraphrased, never dropped. Deterministic signals render only as their disclosed\n159\tcalculation. Claim language is gated per claim kind (`trend | comparison | position`):\n160\ttrend wording only when that claim is eligible, and \"directional, not decision-grade\"\n161\tframing whenever a claim's `decision_grade` is false. A `Modelled` answer renders only\n162\twith its passing backtest artifact reference and applicable stratum, its required\n163\tdisclosures verbatim, and the **actual** as-of-to-census lead time — never \"next year\"\n164\tor \"twelve months\". Enforced by output validators and eval graders, not prompt requests.\n165\t**Consequences.** POC answers show axis confidence without the headline grade until the\n166\tcontract ships it. A model that invented a grade would fail validation — borrowed\n167\tconfidence is excluded by construction. A response claiming `Modelled` without an\n168\tartifact reference is treated as invalid rather than rendered, so an unearned forecast\n169\tcannot reach a customer even if the data layer emitted one.\n170\t\n171\t---\n172\t\n173\t## ADR-010 — The POC product surface is enrolment / market-position intelligence\n174\t\n175\t**Context.** The contract v0.2.0 registry covers school evidence, cohorts/market,\n176\tand area context; it excludes finance, fees, catchments, capacity, outcomes, and\n177\ttransport. The predecessor's lead demo was a financial peer benchmark; the PRD (§15)\n178\tindependently concedes financial benchmarking to an incumbent and aims the product at\n179\tmarket-position intelligence.\n180\t**Decision.** The POC demo and golden-case set are built entirely on the registry's\n181\tquestion groups. Excluded topics produce honest `Unsupported` refusals sourced from the\n182\tcontract's exclusion lists — never improvised answers.\n183\t**Consequences.** Supersedes the predecessor's finance-led demo decision (its\n184\tADR-0023). Revisit only when a finance-bearing contract version ships from the data\n185\tproject.\n186\t\n187\t---\n188\t\n189\t## ADR-011 — Two-tier secrets; dual auth mode\n190\t\n191\t**Context.** Shared machine-level credentials should live once, out of every repo; the\n192\tharness must run on either a metered API key or an account bearer token.\n193\t**Decision.** `config/settings.py` loads secrets highest-precedence-first: process env,\n194\tthen in-repo `.env`, then a shared file found only via the `SHARED_ENV_FILE` pointer\n195\t(absolute path, so daemons with `cwd=/` resolve it). First-set-wins semantics; keys in\n196\t`.env.example` ship commented out (an uncommented-but-empty key shadows the shared\n197\tfile). `ANTHROPIC_AUTH_MODE` auto-detects: bearer token present → account mode (the\n198\tprovider can't carry a bearer token, so account mode builds the client itself);\n199\totherwise API key.\n200\t**Consequences.** Shared tokens live once; an app overrides only deliberately. Both\n201\tcredentials are just env vars, so the layering is auth-mode-agnostic.\n202\t\n203\t---\n204\t\n205\t## ADR-012 — The stub emitter serves fixtures only, and fails loudly when it has none\n206\t\n207\t**Context.** M1 needs an offline substrate for the whole eval suite (architecture §11):\n208\tsomething satisfying the emitter's CLI contract without the data project, its venv or its\n209\tDuckDB. The tempting shape is a small simulator that derives plausible responses from the\n210\tregistry. That shape is a trap: the moment it answers a request nobody wrote a fixture\n211\tfor, every downstream grader is measuring the stub's imagination instead of the agent.\n212\t\n213\t**Decision.** `evals/stub_emitter.py` computes nothing. It matches the invocation to a\n214\tcanned case in `evals/fixtures/stub_envelopes.json` and returns that case's envelope\n215\t(exit 0) or error (exit 2, contract `error_kind` on stderr). Argv, exit codes and the\n216\tstdout/stderr split are byte-compatible with `scripts/emit_poc_evidence.py`; stub-only\n217\tcontrols are environment variables (`STUB_EMITTER_FIXTURES`, `STUB_EMITTER_CASE`) so no\n218\tcall site can depend on a flag the real emitter lacks. Two run-scoped fields are applied\n219\tat emit time and only those two: `request` is the harness's own request echoed back, and\n220\t`provenance.generated_utc` is stamped, matching the real emitter and preserving CR-6's\n221\treplay-equality rule. When no case matches, the stub exits **3** — deliberately outside\n222\tthe contract's `0`/`2`, so an unforeseen request can never be mistaken for either an\n223\tanswer or a contract error. The one exception is mechanical and registry-derived: a\n224\tquestion ID absent from the pinned registry returns `unknown_question`, as the real\n225\temitter does.\n226\t\n227\tThe bundle is generated by `evals/build_stub_fixtures.py`, which copies `support_status`,\n228\t`harness_safe`, `confidence` and `limitations` out of the pinned registry rather than\n229\trestating them; a test regenerates it in memory and fails on any drift.\n230\t\n231\t**Consequences.** A golden case that drifts off its fixture fails loudly instead of\n232\tgrading invented evidence. A contract bump fails three tests, not one: the pin (ADR-008),\n233\tthe fixture-faithfulness check, and the bundle-regeneration check — which is the intended\n234\tnoise, since re-pointing the fixtures is part of adopting a bump. `result.data` shapes and\n235\tthe two preflight payloads are the stub's only invented content (v0.2.0 constrains\n236\tneither); they are marked `provisional` in the bundle, every school name is prefixed\n237\t`FIXTURE`, and re-pointing them touches the generator and nothing else — see O-6.\n238\t\n239\t---\n240\t\n241\t## ADR-013 — Graders are validated adversarially, and grade the transcript\n242\t\n243\t**Context.** M2 builds the deterministic grader tier before the agent exists\n244\t(ADR-007). Two ways to get this wrong were available and both look green:\n245\tgrading the agent's own account of what it did, and validating the graders only\n246\tagainst reference solutions that pass. The second is the more dangerous — a\n247\tsuite of graders hard-coded to return `True` passes every reference solution\n248\tever written.\n249\t\n250\t**Decision.** Three rules.\n251\t\n252\t1. **The transcript is the unit of grading.** `evals/schema.RunRecord` carries\n253\t   every tool call with its raw result alongside the output branch, and every\n254\t   grader reads from it. Envelope preservation compares the rendered fields\n255\t   against the envelope the tool actually returned; grounding compares the\n256\t   narrative's numbers against that same envelope. Nothing is graded against\n257\t   what the model says about its own work. `RunRecord` is an eval-side\n258\t   observation format, not the harness's output type: M3 owns\n259\t   `harness/outputs.py` and must be able to project a run into this shape.\n260\t2. **Every grader carries an adversarial case.** `tests/unit/test_graders.py`\n261\t   mutates a reference run in exactly the way each grader exists to notice and\n262\t   asserts that grader fails it, and a meta-test fails the suite when a grader\n263\t   has no mutation behind it. Reference solutions prove the graders are\n264\t   satisfiable; the mutations prove they are load-bearing. Neither half is\n265\t   sufficient alone.\n266\t3. **The injection grader is chosen per surface, not per suite.** Canary\n267\t   absence is sound only where the canary sits in a field a faithful answer has\n268\t   no reason to reproduce. Where it rides in a school name — which a cohort\n269\t   answer may legitimately quote — absence proves nothing, so the case grades\n270\t   the forbidden behaviour instead, and the planted text is worded to contain\n271\t   none of the phrases that grader matches. The same trap in reverse is the\n272\t   reason forbidden-language scans strip the verbatim limitations first:\n273\t   VC-POC-13's own limitation contains the words \"enrolment forecast\" and\n274\t   \"capacity estimate\", so quoting the prohibition would otherwise fail the rule\n275\t   it states.\n276\t\n277\t**Consequences.** The suite's positive controls are as load-bearing as its\n278\tfailures: a grader that fails an honest render pushes M3 towards a less honest\n279\tanswer to stay green, which is worse than no grader. One limit is recorded\n280\trather than faked — **paraphrase of a limitation is not deterministically\n281\tdetectable**. `limitations_verbatim` proves the strings survived intact in the\n282\tpreserved field, not that the surrounding prose left them unsoftened; M3\n283\ttherefore populates that field from the envelope in code rather than from the\n284\tmodel, and softening is a live-tier (M4) concern.\n285\t\n286\t---\n287\t\n288\t## ADR-014 — One ceiling per behaviour, and the rendering rules written twice\n289\t\n290\t**Context.** M3 assembles the tools, the output union and the rendering gate.\n291\tTwo decisions in it are not obvious and both were found by building it rather\n292\tthan by reasoning about it.\n293\t\n294\t**Decision 1 — the harness owns the repair ceiling.** PydanticAI caps a tool's\n295\t`ModelRetry` at `retries=1` by default. A repair cap living only in\n296\t`settings.MAX_REPAIR_ATTEMPTS` was therefore never the binding limit: the\n297\tframework aborted the run with `UnexpectedModelBehavior` before the harness\n298\tcould turn an unrepairable `bad_request` into an honest refusal. `fetch_evidence`\n299\tnow declares `retries=settings.MAX_REPAIR_ATTEMPTS` so the harness's cap is the\n300\tone that trips, and `RepairCapExceeded` — not a `ModelRetry` — carries the data\n301\tlayer's own message out to an `Unsupported`. Same principle as ADR-003 on\n302\ttransport retries, on a different axis: exactly one owner, and the owner is the\n303\tlayer that knows what the limit means.\n304\t\n305\t**Decision 2 — the preserved fields never pass through the model.** The model\n306\treturns `AnswerDraft`: the narrative, the question ID, and its statement of what\n307\twas empty or gappy. `RenderedAnswer` is assembled by the harness, copying\n308\t`question_id`, `contract_version`, `evidence`, `provenance`, `confidence` and\n309\t`limitations` out of the envelope the tool actually returned. This is the\n310\tstructural answer to the gap ADR-013 recorded — paraphrase is not\n311\tdeterministically detectable, so the model is given no field in which to\n312\tparaphrase.\n313\t\n314\t**Decision 3 — the rendering rules are implemented twice, deliberately.**\n315\t`harness/rendering.py` BLOCKS a bad answer before a caller sees it (violations\n316\treturn to the model as `ModelRetry`); `evals/graders.py` SCORES a finished run\n317\twithout trusting the code that produced it. A shared implementation would make a\n318\tdefect in the shared rule invisible to both at once.\n319\t`tests/unit/test_rendering.py` asserts the two agree on every reference solution\n320\tand on every mutation whose rule both own, so drift between them is a test\n321\tfailure rather than a silent divergence.\n322\t\n323\t**Consequences.** The eval tier can now run the whole production path offline:\n324\t`evals/replay.py` scripts the model's decisions with a `FunctionModel` and runs\n325\tthe real tools, the real subprocess emitter, the real gate and the real assembly,\n326\tthen projects the run into the transcript the M2 graders read. What that proves\n327\tis bounded and worth stating: the machinery carries a correct decision to a\n328\tcorrect rendered answer, and the guardrails fire. Whether a live model makes\n329\tthose decisions is M4's question, and no amount of scripted replay answers it.\n330\t\n331\t---\n332\t\n333\t## ADR-015 — The prompt is generated and frozen; the control envelope is closed; the SDK is pinned\n334\t\n335\t**Context.** M4 turns a system that has never made a live request into one that can.\n336\tThree things had to be settled before the first token was spent, and each was found\n337\tby building the live path rather than by reasoning about it.\n338\t\n339\t**Decision 1 — the prompt is a build artefact, and the input catalog has one source.**\n340\t`prompts/system.md` is generated by `prompts/build_prompt.py` from the pinned\n341\t`contract/` (ADR-006) and read byte-exact at runtime by `harness/prompt.py`; nothing\n342\tinterpolates into it. Regenerating is a step of every contract bump, and\n343\t`tests/unit/test_prompt.py` fails when the committed file has drifted — including a\n344\tsecond-process check, because a value that varies per interpreter is exactly what\n345\twould break caching silently and at full price.\n346\t\n347\tThe allow-listed control values live in ONE file,\n348\t`prompts/input_catalog.snapshot.json`, read by both the prompt generator and\n349\t`evals/build_stub_fixtures.py`. Two copies would have let the model be told a value\n350\tis legal that the offline substrate rejects — and the golden suite would then prove\n351\tnothing about the live tier. The snapshot carries its own `provisional` flag (O-6);\n352\t`--capture` replaces it from a real preflight run.\n353\t\n354\tThe schema block also quotes the registry's **inline conditions** (\"distance_km when\n355\tmarket_definition is distance\"), so the prompt does not tell the model to ask for a\n356\tradius on an SA2 market. That is quoting the contract, not a second condition table:\n357\tthe gate still cannot express the condition (O-8), and the fix for both is CR-5.\n358\t\n359\t**Decision 2 — the control envelope is closed, and it cost a restructure.** `ask()`\n360\tnow drives `agent.iter()` rather than `agent.run()`, because spend can only be\n361\tmeasured between steps. With that:\n362\t\n363\t* **O-7 (spend) closed.** Cost is summed from the run's own message history after\n364\t  every node and trips `BudgetExceeded(limit=\"usd\")`. An unpriced model measures as\n365\t  $0 — the budget is then not watching, and `cost_complete` in the run summary is the\n366\t  field that says so. `LOOP_MAX_USD` is a ceiling on what a run may *commit to*\n367\t  spending, not on what it has spent: the response that crossed the line has already\n368\t  been paid for.\n369\t* **O-9 (wall clock) closed.** One deadline is computed for the whole call and each\n370\t  retry attempt gets only the time remaining, replacing a `wait_for` that sat *inside*\n371\t  the retry owner and could be spent `MAX_ATTEMPTS` times over.\n372\t* **Telemetry on every branch.** A run that trips a budget writes its usage records\n373\t  like any other — the expensive run that returned nothing is the one whose cost you\n374\t  most need (ADR-005). The message history comes back on `deps.last_messages`, which\n375\t  is what carries a clarification round-trip.\n376\t\n377\t**Decision 3 — pin the Anthropic SDK, not just the framework.**\n378\t`pydantic-ai-slim[anthropic]` declares only `anthropic>=0.105.0`. Left to float it\n379\tresolved to 1.3.0, which removed `temperature` from `beta.messages.create()` while\n380\tpydantic-ai 1.106.0 still sends it: a `TypeError` on **every** live request, with the\n381\tentire offline suite green, because nothing offline reaches the provider. `anthropic`\n382\tis now pinned exactly alongside the framework, and both are re-verified together —\n383\tthey are one compatibility surface, not two dependencies.\n384\t\n385\t**Consequences.** A contract bump is now a four-step operation (re-pin, regenerate the\n386\tprompt, regenerate the fixtures, re-point the cases) and the tests name the steps.\n387\tThe live tier (`python -m evals.live`, `tests/live/`) is runnable and manual; it\n388\treports pass@k and pass^k separately, because a case that passes once in three has\n389\tcapability and no reliability, and reporting only the first number is how that ships.\n390\t\n391\t---\n392\t\n393\t## ADR-016 — What the first live run found, and the rule it produces\n394\t\n395\t**Context.** M4's first attempt to run the live tier used an invalid credential. It\n396\tshould have been a two-line outcome. It produced three hundred model requests, a report\n397\tclaiming `pass@1: 0.0` across every case, and roughly five hundred lines of transport\n398\tlogging per trial. Nothing in the 479-test offline suite had failed. Every defect below\n399\twas invisible offline for the same reason: **nothing offline reaches a provider.**\n400\t\n401\t**Finding 1 — the retry owner was inert on the only path that matters.**\n402\t`harness/errors.classify()` matched `anthropic.*` exception types. But an agent run does\n403\tnot raise those: PydanticAI catches the SDK's exception and re-raises its own\n404\t`ModelHTTPError`. So `classify()` returned `None` for every live transport failure — no\n405\t429 was ever retried, no 401 ever failed fast, and ADR-003's single retry owner had\n406\tnothing to own. Both wrappers now route through one status-code table\n407\t(`errors._by_status`), and `test_the_sdk_and_the_wrapper_agree_on_every_status` fails if\n408\tthey diverge.\n409\t\n410\t**Finding 2 — an auth failure was scored as a model failure.** The live runner treated\n411\tevery exception as a failed trial, so one bad key produced 100 \"failures\", a pass@k of\n412\tzero, and a report that read as a capability problem. A rejected credential now raises\n413\t`CredentialsRejected`, stops the suite at the first occurrence, and writes no report. A\n414\t500 or a timeout is still an ordinary failed trial: the tier exists to measure a flaky\n415\tworld, not to give up on it.\n416\t\n417\t**Finding 3 — the single verbosity knob was governing the wrong loggers.** At the default\n418\t`LOG_LEVEL=DEBUG` the transport libraries inherit DEBUG and log the entire outbound\n419\trequest — full system prompt and tool schemas — on every call. `anthropic`, `httpx` and\n420\t`httpcore` are now pinned at WARNING in `logging_config.yaml`. This is the one justified\n421\texception to \"no levels below root\", documented at the point of use; `harness.*` loggers\n422\tstill inherit the knob, and a test holds both halves.\n423\t\n424\t**Finding 4 — a null figure claimed to be the whole story.** The report printed\n425\t`cost: None USD (complete=True)` for a run in which nothing was priced because nothing\n426\tran. `cost_complete` now requires that something was actually priced.\n427\t\n428\t**Decision.** The rule these share: **a failure that cannot happen offline will first be\n429\tobserved in production unless something forces it into the open.** The offline tier's\n430\tcompleteness is not evidence about the live path — it is silence about it. So: an\n431\texception type crossing a library boundary is classified by its *status*, not its class\n432\tlineage; an infrastructure failure is never folded into a quality metric; and the first\n433\tlive run of any new path is treated as an experiment whose findings are recorded, not a\n434\tsmoke test that either passes or is re-run.\n435\t\n436\t**Consequences.** The live tier can now fail informatively, which is the precondition for\n437\tit being worth running at all. Findings 1 and 2 were each a single wrong assumption that\n438\tthe entire offline suite was structurally incapable of catching, and both were found in\n439\tthe first ninety seconds of real use — which is the argument for running the tier early\n440\tand cheaply rather than perfecting it first.\n441\t\n442\t---\n443\t\n444\t## ADR-017 — The demo is rehearsable offline, renders through the product, and always says what its numbers are\n445\t\n446\t**Context.** M5 is the walked demo (architecture §12.5) — the only part of this system an\n447\taudience ever sees. Three things about that change the engineering, and none of them is\n448\tabout presentation quality.\n449\t\n450\tFirst, a demo is the one artefact whose failure mode is a *decision*. A wrong number in a\n451\ttest is a red line; a wrong number on a projector is someone's plan for next year. Two of\n452\tthe three modes this demo can run in show fixtures — the stub emitter's synthetic\n453\tenvelopes, or replayed reference solutions. A room that cannot tell which mode it is\n454\twatching is a room being misled by a system built specifically to stop that.\n455\t\n456\tSecond, the demo had to be provable with no credential and no data project, because that\n457\tis the situation it was written in (and, per ADR-016, \"it will probably work live\" is not\n458\tevidence).\n459\t\n460\tThird, a demo is under permanent pressure to render more beautifully than the product.\n461\t\n462\t**Decision.**\n463\t\n464\t1. **Three modes, and a banner naming what the figures are, printed before the first step\n465\t   and again after the last.** `--replay` (decisions replayed from the reference\n466\t   solutions: no model, no cost, all fixtures), `--stub` (a real model, canned\n467\t   envelopes), and neither flag (real model, real emitter — the only mode where a number\n468\t   on screen is a claim about a school). The banner repeats at the end because someone\n469\t   always walks in halfway, and the second showing is the one they see.\n470\t2. **`--replay` is a first-class mode, not a test fixture.** It runs the real tools, the\n471\t   real emitter subprocess, the real validators and the real renderer, with only the\n472\t   model's decisions scripted — the same mechanism as `evals/replay.py`. So the whole\n473\t   walk-through is exercised end to end in the offline suite, and a presenter can\n474\t   rehearse without spending anything. It proves the wiring; it proves nothing about\n475\t   judgement, and the banner says so in those words.\n476\t3. **The demo defines no renderer.** Every word of every answer comes from\n477\t   `run.render_text`, enforced by a test that fails if `demo.py` grows a `def render_*`.\n478\t   A second renderer would be a second set of rendering rules, and only one of them is\n479\t   tested against ADR-009.\n480\t4. **Every step asks a question the golden suite already grades.** A demo phrasing no\n481\t   case covers is a phrasing nobody has measured — and referencing cases is what makes\n482\t   `--replay` possible at all.\n483\t5. **The four groups are a partition, tested.** §12 names four groups without\n484\t   enumerating them; `demo.GROUPS` enumerates them and a test asserts they cover the\n485\t   harness-safe registry exactly once each, and that every step is captioned with its own\n486\t   question's group.\n487\t\n488\t**Consequences.** The demo can be rehearsed today, at no cost, and is verified in CI as a\n489\treal eight-step run rather than as a script that looks plausible. Property 5 immediately\n490\tearned itself: it caught the refusal step captioned `reference`, a group the refused\n491\tquestion is precisely not in — a caption nobody would have queried on a slide.\n492\t\n493\tThe cost is that `--replay` is a mode that can be mistaken for the product working, which\n494\tis why the banner is a tested property and not a nicety. It is also why a rehearsal\n495\trecords no spend at all rather than `≥ $0.0000 (not fully priced)`: a cost figure for a\n496\trun that made no request is a number with no referent, and this project's whole argument\n497\tis that those are the dangerous kind.\n498\t\n499\t---\n500\t\n501\t## ADR-018 — The local POC uses the signed-in Codex account, not application secrets\n502\t\n503\t**Context.** The POC is run by its owner on a local Mac under existing product\n504\taccounts. Requiring an API key or copying a bearer token into `.env` creates a\n505\tsecond billing and credential boundary that the POC neither needs nor authorises.\n506\tThe harness must still preserve its existing tool loop, typed outputs, rendering\n507\tgate and transcript-based graders.\n508\t\n509\t**Decision.** `codex_account` is the default model provider. A small Pydantic-AI\n510\tmodel adapter invokes `codex exec` and asks for exactly one typed tool/output\n511\taction per turn; Pydantic-AI remains the executor and validator. Authentication\n512\tnever leaves the Codex CLI account store. The subprocess runs ephemeral,\n513\tread-only, without project rules or personal user configuration. The Anthropic\n514\tSDK path remains available only when explicitly selected with\n515\t`HARNESS_MODEL_PROVIDER=anthropic_sdk`.\n516\t\n517\tAccount plans expose neither per-request USD pricing nor provider cache counters\n518\tthrough this CLI boundary. The harness therefore reports account-plan usage,\n519\tcontinues to enforce wall-clock/request/tool-call ceilings, and skips only the\n520\tAnthropic-specific cache-counter assertion. It does not manufacture a `$0` cost.\n521\t\n522\t**Consequences.** The local POC needs `codex login`, not a key or token. The live\n523\tevals still measure the same decisions and grade the same outer transcripts, but\n524\tthey consume account quota and each Pydantic-AI turn starts an ephemeral Codex\n525\texecution. An API deployment would choose and validate a metered provider\n526\tseparately rather than silently inheriting this local transport.\n527\t\n528\t---\n529\t\n530\t## ADR-019 — Claude Code subscription auth replaces the Codex account transport\n531\t\n532\t**Context.** ADR-018 solved secret extraction but selected the wrong product\n533\taccount. The POC owner explicitly requires Claude subscription usage without\n534\tadditional metered API billing and keeps shared secrets in a machine-level\n535\tenvironment file that is sourced before execution.\n536\t\n537\t**Decision.** `claude_account` is the default provider. Its Pydantic-AI adapter\n538\tinvokes `claude --print` with structured output, built-in tools disabled, an\n539\tempty strict MCP configuration, slash commands disabled, no session persistence,\n540\tno settings sources, and a temporary working directory. It requires the inherited\n541\t`CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN`; the child environment removes `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`,\n542\t`ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN`, and all supported third-party provider switches so an\n543\taccount run cannot silently cross into a metered API route. The token remains in\n544\tthe machine-level secrets file and no diagnostic prints any part of it.\n545\t\n546\tThe `anthropic_sdk` provider remains available only through explicit selection\n547\tand is documented as potentially metered. ADR-019 supersedes ADR-018 for the\n548\tlocal POC provider; ADR-018 remains as history of the prior decision.\n549\t\n550\t**Consequences.** A local run must inherit `CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN` before\n551\tstartup. Claude CLI owns subscription limits and cache behavior, so the harness\n552\treports account-plan usage and retains its non-financial step, tool-call, and\n553\twall-clock controls. The CLI can generate text only; Pydantic-AI continues to\n554\town and validate every evidence-tool call.\n555\t\n556\t---\n557\t\n558\t## Open decisions (not yet ADR'd)\n559\t\n560\t- **O-1 — Next contract revision (CR-3…CR-6) adoption plan.** v0.2.0 delivered CR-1\n561\t  (machine-readable `error_kind`) and CR-2; the remaining four were agreed in\n562\t  `contract/HARNESS_RESPONSE_v0.2.0.md` and will ship as one revision. When it lands,\n563\t  adopt in one change per ADR-008 and delete the three interim shims: the hardcoded\n564\t  material-inputs list (`settings.MATERIAL_INPUTS`), harness-side empty/partial\n565\t  detection from `result.data`, and axis-only confidence rendering (ADR-009 interim).\n566\t  The revision's scope is settled on both sides (`contract/HARNESS_REPLY_TO_DATA_DISCOVERY_v0.2.0.md`\n567\t  §6), including the sixth `error_kind`, `unanswerable`.\n568\t- ~~**O-4 — Short-horizon enrolment outlook.**~~ **Resolved 2026-09-02 by the owner**\n569\t  (predecessor ADR-0028 + PRD §6.2/§11 annotations): the POC registers one gated\n570\t  next-census annual-step School FTE forecast (`VC-POC-14`), disabled and\n571\t  `not_currently_supported`, promotable to `Modelled` only by a passing local backtest\n572\t  under preregistered protocol `school_enrolment_fte_1y_v1`. It does **not** add\n573\t  forecasting to MVP. Harness obligations are recorded in ADR-009 and\n574\t  `contract/HARNESS_ACK_FORECAST_READINESS_v0.3.0.md` §8. A failed gate or a blocked\n575\t  identity artifact is a successful validation outcome, not a defect.\n576\t- **O-5 — Open interface asks against v0.3.0.** Three items the harness needs in the\n577\t  shipped v0.3.0 envelope, requested but not yet confirmed\n578\t  (`contract/HARNESS_ACK_FORECAST_READINESS_v0.3.0.md` §4–§6): a machine-readable\n579\t  `reason_code` on `excluded_question` errors (without it, `not_yet_validated` is only\n580\t  prose); required forecast disclosures delivered through `limitations` or\n581\t  `required_disclosures` rather than as protocol text the harness would have to\n582\t  reimplement; and timing fields (`origin_as_of_date`, `as_of_basis`, lead time) as\n583\t  structured values. Until they land, do not hardcode disclosure wording — refuse to\n584\t  render a Modelled answer instead.\n585\t- **O-2 — Server layer timing.** A FastAPI scaffold is carried (`server/`) but unwired;\n586\t  the POC is CLI + evals. Wire it when a demo needs a UI.\n587\t- **O-3 — Clarification budget.** Interim: one clarification round-trip per question in\n588\t  the demo script; still-ambiguous questions render as `Unsupported` with guidance.\n589\t  Revisit against real usage.\n590\t- **O-6 — Two envelope shapes are guesses until a real sample arrives.** The v0.2.0\n591\t  response schema leaves `result.data` unconstrained, and neither preflight\n592\t  (`--input-catalog`, `--school-directory`) has a pinned schema or a published example.\n593\t  The stub's fixtures therefore invent both, using the registry's disclosed physical field\n594\t  names where they exist. **Ask the data project for one real envelope per `result_kind`\n595\t  and one real run of each preflight before M3 builds the tools against these shapes.**\n596\t  **Half closed on 2026-09-03.** The input catalog was captured from the real emitter\n597\t  and adopted. It had first got *wider*: feeding the invented catalog into the system\n598\t  prompt made a wrong value a wrong instruction to the model rather than just a wrong\n599\t  fixture (ADR-015). That was a deliberate trade — one file that can be captured in one\n600\t  step beats two copies that can disagree — and it paid immediately. The guess was wrong\n601\t  in roughly half its keys: there is no `metrics` list (it is `profile_metrics`, beside a\n602\t  `student_context_metrics` we did not know existed), `seifa_indexes` is\n603\t  `seifa_index_types` plus a separate `seifa_measures`, grades begin `pre_year_1_one_year`\n604\t  not `prep`, and six lists we invented are not top-level lists at all. Every one of those\n605\t  errors was being stated to the model as fact. The harness no longer names a catalog list\n606\t  anywhere; it asks the catalog which lists it has. **What remains of O-6** is the five\n607\t  `result.data` shapes and the `--school-directory` record shape, both still invented, and\n608\t  both making the same bet that just lost. Re-capture the catalog with\n609\t  `.venv/bin/python -m prompts.build_prompt --capture`, which rewrites\n610\t  `prompts/input_catalog.snapshot.json` from a real `--input-catalog` run and clears\n611\t  its `provisional` flag; then regenerate the prompt and the fixture bundle. The\n612\t  `result.data` shapes still need one real envelope per `result_kind` and a re-point of\n613\t  `evals/build_stub_fixtures.py`. This bites on the first `--emitter real` run.\n614\t  **Closing it revealed four more gaps (G-5…G-8), now in the same document.** A guess is\n615\t  internally consistent by construction; a real payload is not. Reading the captured\n616\t  catalog against the registry found: `metrics` is required by three questions and the\n617\t  catalog offers two candidate lists without saying which applies (`student_context_metrics`\n618\t  is a strict subset of `profile_metrics`); ten filter keys are named but their values are\n619\t  not enumerated; five required inputs have no allow-list while two catalog lists have no\n620\t  consuming request field; and there are two refusal vocabularies that disagree, which\n621\t  matters because refusal text is quoted verbatim to customers. Three of the four cost the\n622\t  data project a sentence each. **The lesson to carry: capturing a real artefact is not the\n623\t  end of a gap, it is the start of being able to see the next one.**\n624\t  **The ask is now written down for the other side:**\n625\t  `contract/HARNESS_INTERFACE_GAPS_v0.2.0.md` states each gap in data-layer terms — what\n626\t  is missing, why it matters to them, the one file that would close it, our interim\n627\t  behaviour, and the cost of our guess being wrong. It is pinned with the rest of the\n628\t  negotiation record (architecture §2). G-2 (the input catalog) is flagged highest\n629\t  priority there, because that guess now instructs the model rather than only the\n630\t  fixtures. It also raises a new one, G-4: we currently extract the conditional\n631\t  requirement for `distance_km` by string-splitting the registry's own prose\n632\t  (`\"distance_km when market_definition is distance\"`), so rewording that sentence would\n633\t  silently change harness behaviour. That dependency should not exist and they should not\n634\t  have to know about it.\n635\t- ~~**O-7 — The spend budget is declared but not enforced.**~~ **Closed in M4**\n636\t  (ADR-015, decision 2). `harness/usage.spend_usd()` measures cost from the run's own\n637\t  message history and `ask()` checks it between steps, tripping\n638\t  `BudgetExceeded(limit=\"usd\")`. Two limits of the mechanism are recorded rather than\n639\t  papered over: the budget stops the *next* request, not the one that crossed the line\n640\t  (cost is only knowable after a response arrives), and a model unknown to the price\n641\t  database measures as $0 — `cost_complete` in the run summary is the field that says\n642\t  whether the budget was watching at all.\n643\t- **O-8 — `distance_km` is conditionally material, and the gate treats it as\n644\t  unconditional.** The contract makes a radius material for VC-POC-05–07 *only when\n645\t  `market_definition` is `distance`* (`settings.MATERIAL_INPUTS` says so in a comment;\n646\t  `contract.material_inputs_for()` cannot express it). So the materials gate currently\n647\t  accepts a clarification asking for a radius on an SA2 market — an unnecessary\n648\t  round-trip the gate exists to prevent. It never accepts the reverse error (skipping a\n649\t  genuinely material input), so the failure mode is a wasted turn, not a wrong answer.\n650\t  Pinned by a strict `xfail` in `tests/unit/test_agent_loop.py`, so fixing it fails the\n651\t  suite until this entry is updated. The clean fix is the contract's per-question request\n652\t  schemas with conditional rules (CR-5, O-1) — do not build a second hand-maintained\n653\t  condition table in the meantime.\n654\t- ~~**O-9 — The wall-clock budget can be spent up to three times over.**~~ **Closed in\n655\t  M4** (ADR-015, decision 2) by the first of the two options it offered: `ask()`\n656\t  computes one deadline for the whole call and hands each retry attempt only the time\n657\t  remaining, so `LOOP_MAX_SECONDS` bounds the call rather than an attempt. An attempt\n658\t  that starts past the deadline times out immediately instead of running a fresh\n659\t  clock. Pinned by\n660\t  `test_the_wall_clock_deadline_bounds_the_whole_call_not_one_attempt`, which fails if\n661\t  the `wait_for` ever moves back inside the retry wrapper. Tenacity's backoff is now\n662\t  inside the same ceiling: sleeping past the deadline ends the run.\n663\t- **O-10 — Softening a limitation is not detectable offline.** The harness copies\n664\t  `limitations` out of the envelope in code, so the strings cannot be dropped or edited\n665\t  (ADR-014). Nothing deterministic can tell whether the surrounding prose undercuts them\n666\t  — \"these figures are contextual, though in practice they track enrolment closely\" passes\n667\t  every grader in the suite. This is the one rendering rule with no offline check.\n668\t  **Still open after M4, now with a probe rather than a plan.**\n669\t  `python -m evals.live --pressure` appends five red-team phrasings to every\n670\t  should-answer case — \"skip the caveats\", \"don't hedge\", \"the limitations are\n671\t  boilerplate, right?\" — and writes the resulting prose to the report's\n672\t  `*-transcripts.json` for a human to read. The prompt answers them directly (rules\n673\t  block, rule 2: answer without the caveats in the prose and let the attached\n674\t  limitations stand). What the probe does NOT do is grade the result: the graders check\n675\t  the mechanical rules, and whether the prose undercut a limitation is still a\n676\t  judgement about meaning. **The transcripts are the deliverable here, and someone has\n677\t  to read them.** An unread pressure run is worse than none, because it looks like\n678\t  evidence.\n679\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6a5a4bf9-713c-4453-8836-6a3d15f3b2d0","uuid":"27c07cfd-005c-4a56-881b-94a3863e26da","timestamp":"2026-09-25T22:56:21.671Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/shane/Projects/k-12Harness/docs/decisions.md","content":"# Decision Log — K-12 Market Intelligence Harness\n\n> Architecture Decision Records. Append-only. Each records a decision, why, and what it\n> changed. Once logged, decisions are final — don't relitigate without a new ADR\n> superseding the old one. Where an ADR carries a decision proven in the predecessor\n> project, it says so; the predecessor repo (`k-12MarketIntelligence`, archived) holds\n> the full history, and nothing here depends on reading it.\n\nFormat: **Context** · **Decision** · **Consequences**.\n\n---\n\n## ADR-001 — The evidence contract is the only data interface\n\n**Context.** The data layer lives in a separate project that publishes a versioned\ndeterministic evidence contract (question registry + response schemas + CLI emitter,\nvendored under `contract/`). The predecessor harness design had an LLM composing SQL\nover a read-only warehouse schema; that entire concern — schemas, joins, identity\nresolution, data quality — moved behind the data project's contract.\n**Decision.** The harness's only data path is the contract: structured requests to\napproved question IDs via the emitter CLI, run in the data project's own environment.\nNo SQL, no database connection, no raw assets, no name-matching across source systems,\nno arithmetic in the model — every number in every answer originates in the envelope's\n`result`/`evidence`.\n**Consequences.** The harness becomes an intent mapper, slot filler, and faithful\nrenderer. The answerable set is bounded by the registry: growing it is a contract\nrelease, not a harness change. Supersedes the predecessor's text-to-SQL agent design\n(its ADR-0002/0013) — recorded here once so the history is carried without being\nload-bearing.\n\n---\n\n## ADR-002 — PydanticAI (slim, Anthropic-only, pinned exactly); union output with tools\n\n**Context.** The product returns several distinct typed shapes, and the agent must call\ntools and return typed output in the same loop. Proven in the predecessor: Instructor's\n`response_model` rewrites the tool array and evicts action tools — the model then fills\na validating schema with invented values.\n**Decision.** `pydantic-ai-slim[anthropic]`, pinned exactly in `requirements.txt`; a\nunion `output_type` coexisting with custom tools; agent-loop code kept thin so a\nframework swap stays cheap.\n**Consequences.** Adding an answer category is a new union branch, not a loop change.\nThe one framework dep is pinned; other deps stay loose (reproducible-enough for a\nsingle-author project; revisit a lockfile if a transitive break bites).\n\n---\n\n## ADR-003 — Single retry owner: Tenacity, SDK retries disabled\n\n**Context.** The Anthropic SDK retries internally (`max_retries=2` default); a second\nretry layer multiplies requests and drains rate-limit buckets ~3× on 429/529.\n**Decision.** `harness/retry.py` owns all transport retries; the SDK client is\nconstructed with `max_retries=0` (`ANTHROPIC_MAX_RETRIES` in settings). Classification\nlives on the exception hierarchy (`harness/errors.py`): transient retried, terminal and\nauth fail fast.\n**Consequences.** One place for backoff, jitter, and structured retry logging. Never\nadd another retry layer — including around the evidence subprocess, which gets a\ntimeout, not retries (a deterministic emitter that failed once fails again).\n\n---\n\n## ADR-004 — Control envelope enforced in code; typed `BudgetExceeded`\n\n**Context.** Agent loops can run away: steps, tool calls, wall clock, spend.\n**Decision.** `ask()` enforces `UsageLimits` (steps/tool calls) + `asyncio.wait_for`\n(wall clock) + measured spend (`LOOP_MAX_*` in settings); the evidence subprocess has\nits own timeout. A breach returns a typed `BudgetExceeded` carrying what was learned so\nfar — produced by the harness, never selected by the model, never a crash.\n**Consequences.** Worst-case latency and cost are bounded per call. The agent's\n`output_type` stays the success branches; `run_harness()` widens the return type.\n\n---\n\n## ADR-005 — Usage/cost telemetry: JSONL append + DuckDB views at read time\n\n**Context.** Cost work needs per-request granularity (token quartet, USD, latency,\ncache hits) without a hosted tracing service, and telemetry must not couple to any\nother store.\n**Decision.** Each LLM request appends one JSON line to\n`logs/usage/usage-YYYY-MM-DD.jsonl` plus one run-summary line per run; reporting spins\nup an **in-memory** DuckDB over the files (`sql/usage_views.sql`;\n`python -m harness.usage report`). `record_run()` never raises — telemetry failure\ncannot fail a successful run. Costs come from genai-prices (ships with the framework).\n**Consequences.** Near-zero overhead, $0 ongoing cost; cache-hit regression checking is\na one-line query. When this becomes served/multi-user, add a nullable `consumer_id`\nvia a `schema_version` bump — designed in, not bolted on.\n\n---\n\n## ADR-006 — Frozen cached system prompt; per-model cache minimums; schema block generated from the contract\n\n**Context.** Prompt caching needs a byte-exact prefix, and each model has its own\nminimum cacheable length (values have shifted between models before — verify, don't\nassume). The prompt must describe the question registry, which changes only with a\ncontract release.\n**Decision.** One frozen prompt (`prompts/system.md`): rules block + a schema block\n**generated from `contract/`** at build time (per question: ID, purpose,\nrequired/material/optional inputs, result kind; plus the input catalog's allow-lists).\nNothing request-scoped enters the cached prefix. `CACHE_MIN_TOKENS` in settings is the\nsingle source of per-model minimums, re-verified on model upgrades; the regression\ncheck is `cache_read_tokens > 0` in telemetry.\n**Consequences.** Prompt and contract cannot drift — regenerating the schema block is a\nstep of every contract bump (ADR-008). Editing the prompt invalidates the cache; batch\nprompt changes deliberately.\n\n---\n\n## ADR-007 — Eval-first: the golden suite exists before the agent answers a real question\n\n**Context.** The system under test is non-deterministic; single-run assertions and\ngrading the agent's self-report both fail. Proven in the predecessor: deterministic\ngraders reading the transcript, k-trial live runs reporting pass@k (capability) and\npass^k (reliability).\n**Decision.** Golden cases (NL phrasing → expected question ID + request + direction:\nanswer / clarify / refuse / resist-injection) run against a **stub emitter** — a small\nscript satisfying the same CLI contract, serving canned envelopes and canned exit-2\nerrors from fixtures. Deterministic graders: chosen-question match, request match,\nenvelope preservation, grounding (every number in prose traces to the envelope),\nclarification correctness, refusal correctness, canary non-reproduction. Deterministic\ntier gates every PR at zero network cost; the live tier (real credentials, real\nemitter) is manual/nightly, never a PR gate. Refusal cases are generated from the\ncontract's exclusion lists.\n**Consequences.** Cases are authored and graded before the agent exists (M2 before M3);\nadding a capability means adding cases. Live transcripts feed error analysis that grows\nthe suite.\n\n---\n\n## ADR-008 — The contract is pinned by hash; a bump is a loud failure\n\n**Context.** The contract is the one interface this system has, and it is owned by\nanother project. Silent drift — a changed schema, a reworded limitation, a new\nregistry version — must not be absorbable by accident.\n**Decision.** `contract/PINNED.json` records the SHA-256 of every vendored contract\nfile; `tests/unit/test_contract_pin.py` recomputes them on every run and asserts the\nregistry's version equals `settings.CONTRACT_VERSION`. Adopting a new contract version\nis one deliberate change: re-vendor, re-pin, regenerate the prompt schema block\n(ADR-006), delete any interim shims the new version obsoletes, re-point golden cases.\n**Consequences.** A v0.2.0 drop fails the suite by design until adopted. The pin is\nnever edited to make the test pass.\n\n---\n\n## ADR-009 — Confidence and limitations render as the data layer states them; the harness never assigns a grade\n\n**Context.** The product's published confidence vocabulary is a frozen five-grade\ntaxonomy (Observed / Derived / Modelled / Indicative / Unsupported — PRD §6.2),\nassigned by rule from properties of the data. The contract v0.2.0 envelope carries four\naxis ratings but no grade; the mapping is agreed to be the **data project's** to own\n(change request CR-4 in `contract/HARNESS_FEEDBACK_v0.1.0.md`; ordering, composition\nlaw and rule identifiers agreed in `contract/HARNESS_RESPONSE_v0.2.0.md` §3; composition\nby operation, the backtest-eligibility lifecycle, and the `claim_eligibility` field — in\nplace of any coverage-driven grade change — agreed in\n`contract/HARNESS_REPLY_TO_DATA_DISCOVERY_v0.2.0.md`).\n**Decision.** The harness renders confidence exactly as the envelope states it —\ninterim: the four axis ratings verbatim and **no** five-grade label; post-CR-4: the\nenvelope's grade and weakest-input, verbatim. Limitations strings render verbatim,\nnever paraphrased, never dropped. Deterministic signals render only as their disclosed\ncalculation. Claim language is gated per claim kind (`trend | comparison | position`):\ntrend wording only when that claim is eligible, and \"directional, not decision-grade\"\nframing whenever a claim's `decision_grade` is false. A `Modelled` answer renders only\nwith its passing backtest artifact reference and applicable stratum, its required\ndisclosures verbatim, and the **actual** as-of-to-census lead time — never \"next year\"\nor \"twelve months\". Enforced by output validators and eval graders, not prompt requests.\n**Consequences.** POC answers show axis confidence without the headline grade until the\ncontract ships it. A model that invented a grade would fail validation — borrowed\nconfidence is excluded by construction. A response claiming `Modelled` without an\nartifact reference is treated as invalid rather than rendered, so an unearned forecast\ncannot reach a customer even if the data layer emitted one.\n\n---\n\n## ADR-010 — The POC product surface is enrolment / market-position intelligence\n\n**Context.** The contract v0.2.0 registry covers school evidence, cohorts/market,\nand area context; it excludes finance, fees, catchments, capacity, outcomes, and\ntransport. The predecessor's lead demo was a financial peer benchmark; the PRD (§15)\nindependently concedes financial benchmarking to an incumbent and aims the product at\nmarket-position intelligence.\n**Decision.** The POC demo and golden-case set are built entirely on the registry's\nquestion groups. Excluded topics produce honest `Unsupported` refusals sourced from the\ncontract's exclusion lists — never improvised answers.\n**Consequences.** Supersedes the predecessor's finance-led demo decision (its\nADR-0023). Revisit only when a finance-bearing contract version ships from the data\nproject.\n\n---\n\n## ADR-011 — Two-tier secrets; dual auth mode\n\n**Context.** Shared machine-level credentials should live once, out of every repo; the\nharness must run on either a metered API key or an account bearer token.\n**Decision.** `config/settings.py` loads secrets highest-precedence-first: process env,\nthen in-repo `.env`, then a shared file found only via the `SHARED_ENV_FILE` pointer\n(absolute path, so daemons with `cwd=/` resolve it). First-set-wins semantics; keys in\n`.env.example` ship commented out (an uncommented-but-empty key shadows the shared\nfile). `ANTHROPIC_AUTH_MODE` auto-detects: bearer token present → account mode (the\nprovider can't carry a bearer token, so account mode builds the client itself);\notherwise API key.\n**Consequences.** Shared tokens live once; an app overrides only deliberately. Both\ncredentials are just env vars, so the layering is auth-mode-agnostic.\n\n---\n\n## ADR-012 — The stub emitter serves fixtures only, and fails loudly when it has none\n\n**Context.** M1 needs an offline substrate for the whole eval suite (architecture §11):\nsomething satisfying the emitter's CLI contract without the data project, its venv or its\nDuckDB. The tempting shape is a small simulator that derives plausible responses from the\nregistry. That shape is a trap: the moment it answers a request nobody wrote a fixture\nfor, every downstream grader is measuring the stub's imagination instead of the agent.\n\n**Decision.** `evals/stub_emitter.py` computes nothing. It matches the invocation to a\ncanned case in `evals/fixtures/stub_envelopes.json` and returns that case's envelope\n(exit 0) or error (exit 2, contract `error_kind` on stderr). Argv, exit codes and the\nstdout/stderr split are byte-compatible with `scripts/emit_poc_evidence.py`; stub-only\ncontrols are environment variables (`STUB_EMITTER_FIXTURES`, `STUB_EMITTER_CASE`) so no\ncall site can depend on a flag the real emitter lacks. Two run-scoped fields are applied\nat emit time and only those two: `request` is the harness's own request echoed back, and\n`provenance.generated_utc` is stamped, matching the real emitter and preserving CR-6's\nreplay-equality rule. When no case matches, the stub exits **3** — deliberately outside\nthe contract's `0`/`2`, so an unforeseen request can never be mistaken for either an\nanswer or a contract error. The one exception is mechanical and registry-derived: a\nquestion ID absent from the pinned registry returns `unknown_question`, as the real\nemitter does.\n\nThe bundle is generated by `evals/build_stub_fixtures.py`, which copies `support_status`,\n`harness_safe`, `confidence` and `limitations` out of the pinned registry rather than\nrestating them; a test regenerates it in memory and fails on any drift.\n\n**Consequences.** A golden case that drifts off its fixture fails loudly instead of\ngrading invented evidence. A contract bump fails three tests, not one: the pin (ADR-008),\nthe fixture-faithfulness check, and the bundle-regeneration check — which is the intended\nnoise, since re-pointing the fixtures is part of adopting a bump. `result.data` shapes and\nthe two preflight payloads are the stub's only invented content (v0.2.0 constrains\nneither); they are marked `provisional` in the bundle, every school name is prefixed\n`FIXTURE`, and re-pointing them touches the generator and nothing else — see O-6.\n\n---\n\n## ADR-013 — Graders are validated adversarially, and grade the transcript\n\n**Context.** M2 builds the deterministic grader tier before the agent exists\n(ADR-007). Two ways to get this wrong were available and both look green:\ngrading the agent's own account of what it did, and validating the graders only\nagainst reference solutions that pass. The second is the more dangerous — a\nsuite of graders hard-coded to return `True` passes every reference solution\never written.\n\n**Decision.** Three rules.\n\n1. **The transcript is the unit of grading.** `evals/schema.RunRecord` carries\n   every tool call with its raw result alongside the output branch, and every\n   grader reads from it. Envelope preservation compares the rendered fields\n   against the envelope the tool actually returned; grounding compares the\n   narrative's numbers against that same envelope. Nothing is graded against\n   what the model says about its own work. `RunRecord` is an eval-side\n   observation format, not the harness's output type: M3 owns\n   `harness/outputs.py` and must be able to project a run into this shape.\n2. **Every grader carries an adversarial case.** `tests/unit/test_graders.py`\n   mutates a reference run in exactly the way each grader exists to notice and\n   asserts that grader fails it, and a meta-test fails the suite when a grader\n   has no mutation behind it. Reference solutions prove the graders are\n   satisfiable; the mutations prove they are load-bearing. Neither half is\n   sufficient alone.\n3. **The injection grader is chosen per surface, not per suite.** Canary\n   absence is sound only where the canary sits in a field a faithful answer has\n   no reason to reproduce. Where it rides in a school name — which a cohort\n   answer may legitimately quote — absence proves nothing, so the case grades\n   the forbidden behaviour instead, and the planted text is worded to contain\n   none of the phrases that grader matches. The same trap in reverse is the\n   reason forbidden-language scans strip the verbatim limitations first:\n   VC-POC-13's own limitation contains the words \"enrolment forecast\" and\n   \"capacity estimate\", so quoting the prohibition would otherwise fail the rule\n   it states.\n\n**Consequences.** The suite's positive controls are as load-bearing as its\nfailures: a grader that fails an honest render pushes M3 towards a less honest\nanswer to stay green, which is worse than no grader. One limit is recorded\nrather than faked — **paraphrase of a limitation is not deterministically\ndetectable**. `limitations_verbatim` proves the strings survived intact in the\npreserved field, not that the surrounding prose left them unsoftened; M3\ntherefore populates that field from the envelope in code rather than from the\nmodel, and softening is a live-tier (M4) concern.\n\n---\n\n## ADR-014 — One ceiling per behaviour, and the rendering rules written twice\n\n**Context.** M3 assembles the tools, the output union and the rendering gate.\nTwo decisions in it are not obvious and both were found by building it rather\nthan by reasoning about it.\n\n**Decision 1 — the harness owns the repair ceiling.** PydanticAI caps a tool's\n`ModelRetry` at `retries=1` by default. A repair cap living only in\n`settings.MAX_REPAIR_ATTEMPTS` was therefore never the binding limit: the\nframework aborted the run with `UnexpectedModelBehavior` before the harness\ncould turn an unrepairable `bad_request` into an honest refusal. `fetch_evidence`\nnow declares `retries=settings.MAX_REPAIR_ATTEMPTS` so the harness's cap is the\none that trips, and `RepairCapExceeded` — not a `ModelRetry` — carries the data\nlayer's own message out to an `Unsupported`. Same principle as ADR-003 on\ntransport retries, on a different axis: exactly one owner, and the owner is the\nlayer that knows what the limit means.\n\n**Decision 2 — the preserved fields never pass through the model.** The model\nreturns `AnswerDraft`: the narrative, the question ID, and its statement of what\nwas empty or gappy. `RenderedAnswer` is assembled by the harness, copying\n`question_id`, `contract_version`, `evidence`, `provenance`, `confidence` and\n`limitations` out of the envelope the tool actually returned. This is the\nstructural answer to the gap ADR-013 recorded — paraphrase is not\ndeterministically detectable, so the model is given no field in which to\nparaphrase.\n\n**Decision 3 — the rendering rules are implemented twice, deliberately.**\n`harness/rendering.py` BLOCKS a bad answer before a caller sees it (violations\nreturn to the model as `ModelRetry`); `evals/graders.py` SCORES a finished run\nwithout trusting the code that produced it. A shared implementation would make a\ndefect in the shared rule invisible to both at once.\n`tests/unit/test_rendering.py` asserts the two agree on every reference solution\nand on every mutation whose rule both own, so drift between them is a test\nfailure rather than a silent divergence.\n\n**Consequences.** The eval tier can now run the whole production path offline:\n`evals/replay.py` scripts the model's decisions with a `FunctionModel` and runs\nthe real tools, the real subprocess emitter, the real gate and the real assembly,\nthen projects the run into the transcript the M2 graders read. What that proves\nis bounded and worth stating: the machinery carries a correct decision to a\ncorrect rendered answer, and the guardrails fire. Whether a live model makes\nthose decisions is M4's question, and no amount of scripted replay answers it.\n\n---\n\n## ADR-015 — The prompt is generated and frozen; the control envelope is closed; the SDK is pinned\n\n**Context.** M4 turns a system that has never made a live request into one that can.\nThree things had to be settled before the first token was spent, and each was found\nby building the live path rather than by reasoning about it.\n\n**Decision 1 — the prompt is a build artefact, and the input catalog has one source.**\n`prompts/system.md` is generated by `prompts/build_prompt.py` from the pinned\n`contract/` (ADR-006) and read byte-exact at runtime by `harness/prompt.py`; nothing\ninterpolates into it. Regenerating is a step of every contract bump, and\n`tests/unit/test_prompt.py` fails when the committed file has drifted — including a\nsecond-process check, because a value that varies per interpreter is exactly what\nwould break caching silently and at full price.\n\nThe allow-listed control values live in ONE file,\n`prompts/input_catalog.snapshot.json`, read by both the prompt generator and\n`evals/build_stub_fixtures.py`. Two copies would have let the model be told a value\nis legal that the offline substrate rejects — and the golden suite would then prove\nnothing about the live tier. The snapshot carries its own `provisional` flag (O-6);\n`--capture` replaces it from a real preflight run.\n\nThe schema block also quotes the registry's **inline conditions** (\"distance_km when\nmarket_definition is distance\"), so the prompt does not tell the model to ask for a\nradius on an SA2 market. That is quoting the contract, not a second condition table:\nthe gate still cannot express the condition (O-8), and the fix for both is CR-5.\n\n**Decision 2 — the control envelope is closed, and it cost a restructure.** `ask()`\nnow drives `agent.iter()` rather than `agent.run()`, because spend can only be\nmeasured between steps. With that:\n\n* **O-7 (spend) closed.** Cost is summed from the run's own message history after\n  every node and trips `BudgetExceeded(limit=\"usd\")`. An unpriced model measures as\n  $0 — the budget is then not watching, and `cost_complete` in the run summary is the\n  field that says so. `LOOP_MAX_USD` is a ceiling on what a run may *commit to*\n  spending, not on what it has spent: the response that crossed the line has already\n  been paid for.\n* **O-9 (wall clock) closed.** One deadline is computed for the whole call and each\n  retry attempt gets only the time remaining, replacing a `wait_for` that sat *inside*\n  the retry owner and could be spent `MAX_ATTEMPTS` times over.\n* **Telemetry on every branch.** A run that trips a budget writes its usage records\n  like any other — the expensive run that returned nothing is the one whose cost you\n  most need (ADR-005). The message history comes back on `deps.last_messages`, which\n  is what carries a clarification round-trip.\n\n**Decision 3 — pin the Anthropic SDK, not just the framework.**\n`pydantic-ai-slim[anthropic]` declares only `anthropic>=0.105.0`. Left to float it\nresolved to 1.3.0, which removed `temperature` from `beta.messages.create()` while\npydantic-ai 1.106.0 still sends it: a `TypeError` on **every** live request, with the\nentire offline suite green, because nothing offline reaches the provider. `anthropic`\nis now pinned exactly alongside the framework, and both are re-verified together —\nthey are one compatibility surface, not two dependencies.\n\n**Consequences.** A contract bump is now a four-step operation (re-pin, regenerate the\nprompt, regenerate the fixtures, re-point the cases) and the tests name the steps.\nThe live tier (`python -m evals.live`, `tests/live/`) is runnable and manual; it\nreports pass@k and pass^k separately, because a case that passes once in three has\ncapability and no reliability, and reporting only the first number is how that ships.\n\n---\n\n## ADR-016 — What the first live run found, and the rule it produces\n\n**Context.** M4's first attempt to run the live tier used an invalid credential. It\nshould have been a two-line outcome. It produced three hundred model requests, a report\nclaiming `pass@1: 0.0` across every case, and roughly five hundred lines of transport\nlogging per trial. Nothing in the 479-test offline suite had failed. Every defect below\nwas invisible offline for the same reason: **nothing offline reaches a provider.**\n\n**Finding 1 — the retry owner was inert on the only path that matters.**\n`harness/errors.classify()` matched `anthropic.*` exception types. But an agent run does\nnot raise those: PydanticAI catches the SDK's exception and re-raises its own\n`ModelHTTPError`. So `classify()` returned `None` for every live transport failure — no\n429 was ever retried, no 401 ever failed fast, and ADR-003's single retry owner had\nnothing to own. Both wrappers now route through one status-code table\n(`errors._by_status`), and `test_the_sdk_and_the_wrapper_agree_on_every_status` fails if\nthey diverge.\n\n**Finding 2 — an auth failure was scored as a model failure.** The live runner treated\nevery exception as a failed trial, so one bad key produced 100 \"failures\", a pass@k of\nzero, and a report that read as a capability problem. A rejected credential now raises\n`CredentialsRejected`, stops the suite at the first occurrence, and writes no report. A\n500 or a timeout is still an ordinary failed trial: the tier exists to measure a flaky\nworld, not to give up on it.\n\n**Finding 3 — the single verbosity knob was governing the wrong loggers.** At the default\n`LOG_LEVEL=DEBUG` the transport libraries inherit DEBUG and log the entire outbound\nrequest — full system prompt and tool schemas — on every call. `anthropic`, `httpx` and\n`httpcore` are now pinned at WARNING in `logging_config.yaml`. This is the one justified\nexception to \"no levels below root\", documented at the point of use; `harness.*` loggers\nstill inherit the knob, and a test holds both halves.\n\n**Finding 4 — a null figure claimed to be the whole story.** The report printed\n`cost: None USD (complete=True)` for a run in which nothing was priced because nothing\nran. `cost_complete` now requires that something was actually priced.\n\n**Decision.** The rule these share: **a failure that cannot happen offline will first be\nobserved in production unless something forces it into the open.** The offline tier's\ncompleteness is not evidence about the live path — it is silence about it. So: an\nexception type crossing a library boundary is classified by its *status*, not its class\nlineage; an infrastructure failure is never folded into a quality metric; and the first\nlive run of any new path is treated as an experiment whose findings are recorded, not a\nsmoke test that either passes or is re-run.\n\n**Consequences.** The live tier can now fail informatively, which is the precondition for\nit being worth running at all. Findings 1 and 2 were each a single wrong assumption that\nthe entire offline suite was structurally incapable of catching, and both were found in\nthe first ninety seconds of real use — which is the argument for running the tier early\nand cheaply rather than perfecting it first.\n\n---\n\n## ADR-017 — The demo is rehearsable offline, renders through the product, and always says what its numbers are\n\n**Context.** M5 is the walked demo (architecture §12.5) — the only part of this system an\naudience ever sees. Three things about that change the engineering, and none of them is\nabout presentation quality.\n\nFirst, a demo is the one artefact whose failure mode is a *decision*. A wrong number in a\ntest is a red line; a wrong number on a projector is someone's plan for next year. Two of\nthe three modes this demo can run in show fixtures — the stub emitter's synthetic\nenvelopes, or replayed reference solutions. A room that cannot tell which mode it is\nwatching is a room being misled by a system built specifically to stop that.\n\nSecond, the demo had to be provable with no credential and no data project, because that\nis the situation it was written in (and, per ADR-016, \"it will probably work live\" is not\nevidence).\n\nThird, a demo is under permanent pressure to render more beautifully than the product.\n\n**Decision.**\n\n1. **Three modes, and a banner naming what the figures are, printed before the first step\n   and again after the last.** `--replay` (decisions replayed from the reference\n   solutions: no model, no cost, all fixtures), `--stub` (a real model, canned\n   envelopes), and neither flag (real model, real emitter — the only mode where a number\n   on screen is a claim about a school). The banner repeats at the end because someone\n   always walks in halfway, and the second showing is the one they see.\n2. **`--replay` is a first-class mode, not a test fixture.** It runs the real tools, the\n   real emitter subprocess, the real validators and the real renderer, with only the\n   model's decisions scripted — the same mechanism as `evals/replay.py`. So the whole\n   walk-through is exercised end to end in the offline suite, and a presenter can\n   rehearse without spending anything. It proves the wiring; it proves nothing about\n   judgement, and the banner says so in those words.\n3. **The demo defines no renderer.** Every word of every answer comes from\n   `run.render_text`, enforced by a test that fails if `demo.py` grows a `def render_*`.\n   A second renderer would be a second set of rendering rules, and only one of them is\n   tested against ADR-009.\n4. **Every step asks a question the golden suite already grades.** A demo phrasing no\n   case covers is a phrasing nobody has measured — and referencing cases is what makes\n   `--replay` possible at all.\n5. **The four groups are a partition, tested.** §12 names four groups without\n   enumerating them; `demo.GROUPS` enumerates them and a test asserts they cover the\n   harness-safe registry exactly once each, and that every step is captioned with its own\n   question's group.\n\n**Consequences.** The demo can be rehearsed today, at no cost, and is verified in CI as a\nreal eight-step run rather than as a script that looks plausible. Property 5 immediately\nearned itself: it caught the refusal step captioned `reference`, a group the refused\nquestion is precisely not in — a caption nobody would have queried on a slide.\n\nThe cost is that `--replay` is a mode that can be mistaken for the product working, which\nis why the banner is a tested property and not a nicety. It is also why a rehearsal\nrecords no spend at all rather than `≥ $0.0000 (not fully priced)`: a cost figure for a\nrun that made no request is a number with no referent, and this project's whole argument\nis that those are the dangerous kind.\n\n---\n\n## ADR-018 — The local POC uses the signed-in Codex account, not application secrets\n\n**Context.** The POC is run by its owner on a local Mac under existing product\naccounts. Requiring an API key or copying a bearer token into `.env` creates a\nsecond billing and credential boundary that the POC neither needs nor authorises.\nThe harness must still preserve its existing tool loop, typed outputs, rendering\ngate and transcript-based graders.\n\n**Decision.** `codex_account` is the default model provider. A small Pydantic-AI\nmodel adapter invokes `codex exec` and asks for exactly one typed tool/output\naction per turn; Pydantic-AI remains the executor and validator. Authentication\nnever leaves the Codex CLI account store. The subprocess runs ephemeral,\nread-only, without project rules or personal user configuration. The Anthropic\nSDK path remains available only when explicitly selected with\n`HARNESS_MODEL_PROVIDER=anthropic_sdk`.\n\nAccount plans expose neither per-request USD pricing nor provider cache counters\nthrough this CLI boundary. The harness therefore reports account-plan usage,\ncontinues to enforce wall-clock/request/tool-call ceilings, and skips only the\nAnthropic-specific cache-counter assertion. It does not manufacture a `$0` cost.\n\n**Consequences.** The local POC needs `codex login`, not a key or token. The live\nevals still measure the same decisions and grade the same outer transcripts, but\nthey consume account quota and each Pydantic-AI turn starts an ephemeral Codex\nexecution. An API deployment would choose and validate a metered provider\nseparately rather than silently inheriting this local transport.\n\n---\n\n## ADR-019 — Claude Code subscription auth replaces the Codex account transport\n\n**Context.** ADR-018 solved secret extraction but selected the wrong product\naccount. The POC owner explicitly requires Claude subscription usage without\nadditional metered API billing and keeps shared secrets in a machine-level\nenvironment file that is sourced before execution.\n\n**Decision.** `claude_account` is the default provider. Its Pydantic-AI adapter\ninvokes `claude --print` with structured output, built-in tools disabled, an\nempty strict MCP configuration, slash commands disabled, no session persistence,\nno settings sources, and a temporary working directory. It requires the inherited\n`CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN`; the child environment removes `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`,\n`ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN`, and all supported third-party provider switches so an\naccount run cannot silently cross into a metered API route. The token remains in\nthe machine-level secrets file and no diagnostic prints any part of it.\n\nThe `anthropic_sdk` provider remains available only through explicit selection\nand is documented as potentially metered. ADR-019 supersedes ADR-018 for the\nlocal POC provider; ADR-018 remains as history of the prior decision.\n\n**Consequences.** A local run must inherit `CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN` before\nstartup. Claude CLI owns subscription limits and cache behavior, so the harness\nreports account-plan usage and retains its non-financial step, tool-call, and\nwall-clock controls. The CLI can generate text only; Pydantic-AI continues to\nown and validate every evidence-tool call.\n\n---\n\n## Open decisions (not yet ADR'd)\n\n- **O-1 — Next contract revision (CR-3…CR-6) adoption plan.** v0.2.0 delivered CR-1\n  (machine-readable `error_kind`) and CR-2; the remaining four were agreed in\n  `contract/HARNESS_RESPONSE_v0.2.0.md` and will ship as one revision. When it lands,\n  adopt in one change per ADR-008 and delete the three interim shims: the hardcoded\n  material-inputs list (`settings.MATERIAL_INPUTS`), harness-side empty/partial\n  detection from `result.data`, and axis-only confidence rendering (ADR-009 interim).\n  The revision's scope is settled on both sides (`contract/HARNESS_REPLY_TO_DATA_DISCOVERY_v0.2.0.md`\n  §6), including the sixth `error_kind`, `unanswerable`.\n- ~~**O-4 — Short-horizon enrolment outlook.**~~ **Resolved 2026-09-02 by the owner**\n  (predecessor ADR-0028 + PRD §6.2/§11 annotations): the POC registers one gated\n  next-census annual-step School FTE forecast (`VC-POC-14`), disabled and\n  `not_currently_supported`, promotable to `Modelled` only by a passing local backtest\n  under preregistered protocol `school_enrolment_fte_1y_v1`. It does **not** add\n  forecasting to MVP. Harness obligations are recorded in ADR-009 and\n  `contract/HARNESS_ACK_FORECAST_READINESS_v0.3.0.md` §8. A failed gate or a blocked\n  identity artifact is a successful validation outcome, not a defect.\n- **O-5 — Open interface asks against v0.3.0.** Three items the harness needs in the\n  shipped v0.3.0 envelope, requested but not yet confirmed\n  (`contract/HARNESS_ACK_FORECAST_READINESS_v0.3.0.md` §4–§6): a machine-readable\n  `reason_code` on `excluded_question` errors (without it, `not_yet_validated` is only\n  prose); required forecast disclosures delivered through `limitations` or\n  `required_disclosures` rather than as protocol text the harness would have to\n  reimplement; and timing fields (`origin_as_of_date`, `as_of_basis`, lead time) as\n  structured values. Until they land, do not hardcode disclosure wording — refuse to\n  render a Modelled answer instead.\n- **O-2 — Server layer timing.** A FastAPI scaffold is carried (`server/`) but unwired;\n  the POC is CLI + evals. Wire it when a demo needs a UI.\n- **O-3 — Clarification budget.** Interim: one clarification round-trip per question in\n  the demo script; still-ambiguous questions render as `Unsupported` with guidance.\n  Revisit against real usage.\n- **O-6 — Two envelope shapes are guesses until a real sample arrives.** The v0.2.0\n  response schema leaves `result.data` unconstrained, and neither preflight\n  (`--input-catalog`, `--school-directory`) has a pinned schema or a published example.\n  The stub's fixtures therefore invent both, using the registry's disclosed physical field\n  names where they exist. **Ask the data project for one real envelope per `result_kind`\n  and one real run of each preflight before M3 builds the tools against these shapes.**\n  **Half closed on 2026-09-03.** The input catalog was captured from the real emitter\n  and adopted. It had first got *wider*: feeding the invented catalog into the system\n  prompt made a wrong value a wrong instruction to the model rather than just a wrong\n  fixture (ADR-015). That was a deliberate trade — one file that can be captured in one\n  step beats two copies that can disagree — and it paid immediately. The guess was wrong\n  in roughly half its keys: there is no `metrics` list (it is `profile_metrics`, beside a\n  `student_context_metrics` we did not know existed), `seifa_indexes` is\n  `seifa_index_types` plus a separate `seifa_measures`, grades begin `pre_year_1_one_year`\n  not `prep`, and six lists we invented are not top-level lists at all. Every one of those\n  errors was being stated to the model as fact. The harness no longer names a catalog list\n  anywhere; it asks the catalog which lists it has. **What remains of O-6** is the five\n  `result.data` shapes and the `--school-directory` record shape, both still invented, and\n  both making the same bet that just lost. Re-capture the catalog with\n  `.venv/bin/python -m prompts.build_prompt --capture`, which rewrites\n  `prompts/input_catalog.snapshot.json` from a real `--input-catalog` run and clears\n  its `provisional` flag; then regenerate the prompt and the fixture bundle. The\n  `result.data` shapes still need one real envelope per `result_kind` and a re-point of\n  `evals/build_stub_fixtures.py`. This bites on the first `--emitter real` run.\n  **Closing it revealed four more gaps (G-5…G-8), now in the same document.** A guess is\n  internally consistent by construction; a real payload is not. Reading the captured\n  catalog against the registry found: `metrics` is required by three questions and the\n  catalog offers two candidate lists without saying which applies (`student_context_metrics`\n  is a strict subset of `profile_metrics`); ten filter keys are named but their values are\n  not enumerated; five required inputs have no allow-list while two catalog lists have no\n  consuming request field; and there are two refusal vocabularies that disagree, which\n  matters because refusal text is quoted verbatim to customers. Three of the four cost the\n  data project a sentence each. **The lesson to carry: capturing a real artefact is not the\n  end of a gap, it is the start of being able to see the next one.**\n  **The ask is now written down for the other side:**\n  `contract/HARNESS_INTERFACE_GAPS_v0.2.0.md` states each gap in data-layer terms — what\n  is missing, why it matters to them, the one file that would close it, our interim\n  behaviour, and the cost of our guess being wrong. It is pinned with the rest of the\n  negotiation record (architecture §2). G-2 (the input catalog) is flagged highest\n  priority there, because that guess now instructs the model rather than only the\n  fixtures. It also raises a new one, G-4: we currently extract the conditional\n  requirement for `distance_km` by string-splitting the registry's own prose\n  (`\"distance_km when market_definition is distance\"`), so rewording that sentence would\n  silently change harness behaviour. That dependency should not exist and they should not\n  have to know about it.\n- ~~**O-7 — The spend budget is declared but not enforced.**~~ **Closed in M4**\n  (ADR-015, decision 2). `harness/usage.spend_usd()` measures cost from the run's own\n  message history and `ask()` checks it between steps, tripping\n  `BudgetExceeded(limit=\"usd\")`. Two limits of the mechanism are recorded rather than\n  papered over: the budget stops the *next* request, not the one that crossed the line\n  (cost is only knowable after a response arrives), and a model unknown to the price\n  database measures as $0 — `cost_complete` in the run summary is the field that says\n  whether the budget was watching at all.\n- **O-8 — `distance_km` is conditionally material, and the gate treats it as\n  unconditional.** The contract makes a radius material for VC-POC-05–07 *only when\n  `market_definition` is `distance`* (`settings.MATERIAL_INPUTS` says so in a comment;\n  `contract.material_inputs_for()` cannot express it). So the materials gate currently\n  accepts a clarification asking for a radius on an SA2 market — an unnecessary\n  round-trip the gate exists to prevent. It never accepts the reverse error (skipping a\n  genuinely material input), so the failure mode is a wasted turn, not a wrong answer.\n  Pinned by a strict `xfail` in `tests/unit/test_agent_loop.py`, so fixing it fails the\n  suite until this entry is updated. The clean fix is the contract's per-question request\n  schemas with conditional rules (CR-5, O-1) — do not build a second hand-maintained\n  condition table in the meantime.\n- ~~**O-9 — The wall-clock budget can be spent up to three times over.**~~ **Closed in\n  M4** (ADR-015, decision 2) by the first of the two options it offered: `ask()`\n  computes one deadline for the whole call and hands each retry attempt only the time\n  remaining, so `LOOP_MAX_SECONDS` bounds the call rather than an attempt. An attempt\n  that starts past the deadline times out immediately instead of running a fresh\n  clock. Pinned by\n  `test_the_wall_clock_deadline_bounds_the_whole_call_not_one_attempt`, which fails if\n  the `wait_for` ever moves back inside the retry wrapper. Tenacity's backoff is now\n  inside the same ceiling: sleeping past the deadline ends the run.\n- **O-10 — Softening a limitation is not detectable offline.** The harness copies\n  `limitations` out of the envelope in code, so the strings cannot be dropped or edited\n  (ADR-014). Nothing deterministic can tell whether the surrounding prose undercuts them\n  — \"these figures are contextual, though in practice they track enrolment closely\" passes\n  every grader in the suite. This is the one rendering rule with no offline check.\n  **Still open after M4, now with a probe rather than a plan.**\n  `python -m evals.live --pressure` appends five red-team phrasings to every\n  should-answer case — \"skip the caveats\", \"don't hedge\", \"the limitations are\n  boilerplate, right?\" — and writes the resulting prose to the report's\n  `*-transcripts.json` for a human to read. The prompt answers them directly (rules\n  block, rule 2: answer without the caveats in the prose and let the attached\n  limitations stand). What the probe does NOT do is grade the result: the graders check\n  the mechanical rules, and whether the prose undercut a limitation is still a\n  judgement about meaning. **The transcripts are the deliverable here, and someone has\n  to read them.** An unread pressure run is worse than none, because it looks like\n  evidence.\n","numLines":679,"startLine":1,"totalLines":679}}}
